   "Cycling also cleans the air. Every short journey made by bike rather than car means fewer exhaust fumes and lower carbon emissions, and in dense city centres this can make a noticeable difference to pollution levels.",
   "Health is another powerful motive. Regular cycling builds fitness, reduces the risk of heart disease and improves mental well-being, which in turn eases pressure on hospitals. For residents, a bicycle is also far cheaper to buy and run than a car, and for journeys of a few kilometres it is often faster, since cyclists avoid traffic jams and never have to search for a parking space.",
   "Cities themselves save money: cycle lanes cost a fraction of new roads to build and maintain. Streets with fewer cars are quieter and more pleasant, and several studies suggest that local shops benefit, because cyclists stop more often than drivers who speed past.",
   "Not everyone is convinced. Some drivers complain of a ‘war on motorists’ as lanes are narrowed and parking removed. Their frustration is understandable, but it is misplaced: every person on a bike is one fewer car in the queue ahead of them. Cycling, it seems, may be the rare policy where almost everyone wins.",
